// The explanations of the own pass's codes (0.17, front/own/diag.vl;
// docs/design/value-semantics.md §2.9, §4): what each reports, the fix
// with its replacement text (agents fix by hand more often than they run a
// try: line), and an example: the code, then the diagnostic as `check`
// prints it, split at its ` | `. The tasks that report them: T1 moved,
// move_out, partial, drop_view; T2 conflict, escape, static, readonly; T3
// views. tests/own-try runs the try: lines of these examples.

use crate::tools::explain::Explain;

pub const OWN_COUNT: i64 = 9;

pub static OWN_TABLE: [Explain; 9] = [
    Explain {
        code: "own.moved",
        severity: "error (--values=error, the default; a warning with warn, a note with report)",
        means: "A value of an owned type (one with a Drop, or holding a Vec, Buf or HashMap) is \
            used after it moved. `let w = v` of a whole local, an argument to a `sink` \
            parameter, a struct literal's field, `return v` and a `sink ||` capture all move \
            it: the old name is dead until it is assigned again. Plain values (numbers, \
            structs of them) copy and never move.",
        fix: "When the callee only reads it, make the parameter let, `v: Vec<T>` (no marker at \
            the call: `f(v)` lends it for the call); `volec refactor signature F --convention \
            v let` changes the parameter and its calls. When it changes it, `v: inout Vec<T>`, \
            called `f(&v)` (`--convention v inout`). Else pass a copy, `f(v.clone())`, or use \
            the value before the move.",
        example: "fn total(v: sink Vec<i64>) -> i64 { var s = 0; for x in v { s += x; } s }\n\
            ...\n\
            let n = total(v);\n\
            v.push(n);\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:6:5: error[own.moved] 'v' is used here after it moved into 'total(sink v)' at 5:19\n\
            \x20 help: use it before the move, pass a copy where it moves ('.clone()'), or let the callee borrow it: a let parameter ('x: T'), not 'sink'\n\
            \x20 try: volec edit patch main.vl:5 --old 'let n = total(v);' --new 'let n = total(v.clone());'",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "own.move_out",
        severity: "error (as own.moved)",
        means: "An owned value is moved out of a place that must keep one: an index (`v[i]`), a \
            static, or a part of an access (`self.keys` in an `inout self` method, a field of \
            a let or inout parameter). The place would be left without a value, and its owner \
            would drop it again.",
        fix: "Take it out and leave an empty one: `self.keys.take()` (a Vec, Buf or HashMap), \
            `std::take(&self.keys)`; swap a new value in: `std::replace(&self.keys, new)`; \
            remove an element: `v.remove(i)`; or copy it: `self.keys.clone()`. To only read \
            it, borrow it: `let ks = self.keys;` (a let binding of a part borrows), `for k in \
            self.keys`, or pass it to a let parameter.",
        example: "fn rehash(inout self) {\n\
            \x20   var old = self.keys;\n\
            \x20   for k in old { self.insert(k); }\n\
            }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:5:19: error[own.move_out] cannot move 'self.keys' (Vec<i64>) out of a reference: what it points to keeps its value\n\
            \x20 help: take it out and leave an empty value in its place ('x.take()' of a Vec, Buf or HashMap, 'std::take(&x)', 'std::replace(&x, v)'), copy it ('.clone()'), or borrow it: pass it on let, or take the parameter 'sink' to own it\n\
            \x20 try: volec edit patch main.vl:5 --old 'var old = self.keys;' --new 'var old = self.keys.take();'\n\
            \x20 try: volec edit patch main.vl:5 --old 'var old = self.keys;' --new 'var old = self.keys.clone();'",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "own.partial",
        severity: "error (as own.moved)",
        means: "A field is moved out of a value whose type implements Drop: its `drop` would run \
            on a value with the field gone. Moving fields out of a struct without its own Drop \
            is allowed (a partial move; the rest is dropped field by field).",
        fix: "Take the field out and leave an empty one, `std::take(&f.name)`, or copy it, \
            `f.name.clone()`; or move the whole value.",
        example: "impl Drop for File { fn drop(inout self) { close(self.fd); } }\n\
            let name = f.name;\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:5:16: error[own.partial] cannot move 'f.name' out of 'f': 'File' implements Drop, which needs all of it\n\
            \x20 help: take the field out and leave a value in its place ('std::take(&x.f)', 'std::replace(&x.f, v)'), or copy it ('.clone()')\n\
            \x20 try: volec edit patch main.vl:5 --old 'let name = f.name;' --new 'let name = std::take(&f.name);'",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "own.conflict",
        severity: "error (as own.moved)",
        means: "A place is written, moved, dropped or lent inout (`&x`) while a view or an \
            access of it is used later (aliasing XOR mutation, checked within the function): \
            a slice of a Vec used after a push (the push may move the elements); a `for` loop \
            over a Vec that pushes to it; two arguments of one call where one is `&a` (inout) \
            and another views `a` (`f(&a, a[..])`, `f(&v[0], v)`; a read finished before the \
            call, `f(&a, a.len())`, is fine); an `inout self` method whose arguments change \
            the receiver (`rng.big(rng.next())`); a closure that changes a capture, while \
            that capture is used another way before the closure's last use, or called or \
            passed alongside an access of it (`f(&n)` for an `f` capturing n); a view \
            returned by a method of a type that owns the text (`views(self)`) kept across the \
            next call. The line names both positions; when a callee causes it, it names the \
            callee's view summary and the line that makes it.",
        fix: "Use the view before the change, or take it again after (`let s = v.as_slice();` \
            after the push); keep an index instead of a view; copy a plain element out first \
            (`let x = v[0];`); loop over indexes (`for i in 0..v.len() { let x = v[i]; ... }`) \
            or collect the new elements and append them after the loop; hoist an argument \
            that changes the receiver into a `let`; `v.swap(i, j)` for two elements. A \
            closure: move the other use after its last call, or pass the value to it as a \
            parameter instead of capturing it. A lexer that owns its text and returns views \
            of it: let it view text its caller owns (`src: [u8]`), or copy each word. `volec \
            query own FILE:LINE:COL` shows every local's state there.",
        example: "let first = v.as_slice();\n\
            v.push(4);\n\
            println(f\"{first[0]}\");\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:5:5: error[own.conflict] 'v.push(4)' may move v's elements while 'first' (4:17) views them; 'first' is used at 6:16\n\
            \x20 help: use 'first' before the push, keep an index instead of a view, or keep a copy\n\
            \x20 try: volec edit patch main.vl:4 --old 'v.as_slice()' --new 'v.clone()'\n\
            \x20 try: volec query own main.vl:6:16\n\
            \n\
            for x in v { if x % 2 == 0 { v.push(x / 2); } }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:7:30: error[own.conflict] 'v.push' may move v's elements while the 'for' at 7:1 iterates over them\n\
            \x20 try: volec edit patch main.vl:7 --old 'for x in v {' --new 'for i in 0..v.len() { let x = v[i];'",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "own.escape",
        severity: "error (as own.moved)",
        means: "A view outlives what it points into: a function returns a slice (or a struct \
            holding one) of a local, or of an owned parameter, which is dropped when it \
            returns; a block's value views a local of the block; a view is stored into a \
            static or into what an inout parameter designates, which outlives its owner.",
        fix: "Return the owner (the Buf, the Vec) instead of a view of it; a caller that wants \
            a view takes `.as_slice()` of what it got. Or take the text as a parameter (`s: \
            [u8]`), so that the result views the caller's text. A function that returns a view \
            of its parameter (`fn first_word(s: [u8]) -> [u8]`) is fine.",
        example: "fn greet(name: [u8]) -> [u8] {\n\
            \x20   let b = format(f\"hello {name}\");\n\
            \x20   b.as_slice()\n\
            }\n\
            \n\
            greet.vl:3:5: error[own.escape] 'greet' returns a view of 'b', which is dropped when 'greet' returns\n\
            \x20 help: return the Buf itself; a caller that wants a view takes '.as_slice()' of it\n\
            \x20 try: volec edit patch greet --old '-> [u8]' --new '-> Buf' --old 'b.as_slice()' --new 'b'",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "own.static",
        severity: "error (as own.moved)",
        means: "A view of a static (`TYPES.as_slice()`, `TABLE[..]`) is used after a call whose \
            effects include `global` (it may write statics, and so grow or free what the view \
            points into); or a static is lent (`&S`, or let) to such a function.",
        fix: "Copy the plain value out first (`let t = TYPES[i];`), or take the view again \
            after the call. `volec query effects F --why global` shows why the callee \
            writes statics.",
        example: "let t = TYPES.as_slice();\n\
            let id = intern(name);\n\
            let k = t[i];\n\
            \n\
            types.vl:41:9: error[own.static] 'intern' writes statics (global) while 't' (40:9) views TYPES; 't' is used at 42:13\n\
            \x20 help: copy the plain value first: 'let t = TYPES[i];'\n\
            \x20 try: volec query effects intern --why global",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "own.views",
        severity: "error",
        means: "A `#[views(...)]` or `#[stores(...)]` declaration is wrong: it names something \
            that is not a parameter (`p`) or what a parameter's views point into (`*p`); or \
            the body returns or stores views of more than it declares (the declaration pins \
            the summary its callers rely on).",
        fix: "Name the parameters the result points into: `#[views(self)]` for a view of what \
            `self` points to (`self.src.as_slice()[a..b]`), `#[views(*self)]` for a view \
            `self` holds (a token's text out of `self.toks`). `volec query views NAME \
            --why` shows the inferred summary and the line making each source; removing the \
            declaration lets it be inferred.",
        example: "#[views(*self)]\n\
            fn word(self) -> [u8] { self.src.as_slice()[0..1] }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:4:29: error[own.views] 'Lexer::word' is #[views(*self)] but returns a view of 'self': 'self.src.as_slice()[0..1]'\n\
            \x20 help: callers rely on the declaration: change the body to keep to it, or declare what it does: #[views(self)]\n\
            \x20 try: volec query views Lexer::word --why",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "own.drop_view",
        severity: "error",
        means: "A view type (one holding a slice, an access, or a closure capturing by \
            reference) implements Drop. Its drop could read what its owner already freed, so \
            view types have none: this is what keeps vole free of Rust's drop-check rules.",
        fix: "Implement Drop on the owner instead (the type holding the Buf or Vec), or make \
            the type own its data (`text: Buf` rather than `text: [u8]`).",
        example: "struct Scanner { text: [u8], pos: i64 }\n\
            impl Drop for Scanner { fn drop(inout self) { ... } }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:2:25: error[own.drop_view] 'Scanner' holds views and cannot implement Drop: its drop could read what it views after that is gone\n\
            \x20 help: hold owned values (Buf, Vec) instead of the views, or remove the impl",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "own.readonly",
        severity: "error (as own.moved)",
        means: "A change of something read-only. (0.25) An inout access of a let place: `&x` \
            given to an inout parameter, `inout r = &x`, `match &x`, or an `inout self` method \
            (`x.push(1)`, `x[i] = v` on a Vec) whose root is a let binding, a let parameter \
            (a copy of a plain value), a let receiver (`self` of a `fn m(self)`), or an \
            immutable static (in read-only memory). Also a write through a let access or a \
            let slice (`[T]`): an assignment, an inout method, a let parameter of an owned \
            type changed; a change to an owned copy that borrows (a `match` binding, a \
            `v.get(i)`, a `for` loop's element), whose original keeps pointing at what the \
            change may free; and a let `[T]` given where `inout [T]` is expected, to a callee \
            that writes through it (`sort(v.as_slice())`, a store intrinsic). `&x` given \
            where a raw `*T` is wanted is its address and needs no var.",
        fix: "A local: declare it `var n = ...`. A parameter: take it `x: inout T` and pass \
            `&x` at the calls (`volec refactor signature F --convention x inout`), or `x: \
            sink T` for a copy of its own. A receiver: `inout self`. A static: `static var \
            N`. A slice: `inout [T]`, passed as `&v`, `&a[..]` or `v.as_mut_slice()`.",
        example: "let n = 1;\n\
            bump(&n);\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:4:10: error[own.readonly] '&n' changes 'n', a let binding: it is read-only\n\
            \x20 help: declare it `var` to change it\n\
            \x20 try: var n = ...\n\
            \n\
            fn reset(p: P) { p.x.clear(); }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:2:18: error[own.readonly] writes through 'p', a 'let P', which is read-only (let: borrowed to be read)\n\
            \x20 help: take it inout ('x: inout T', called with '&x') to change it\n\
            \x20 try: volec refactor signature reset --type p 'inout P'",
    },
];
